[Event "Black openings: he plays the king pawn"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/InPnxGYF"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today you play black. Whatever he grabs in the middle, you grab straight back. }
1. e4 { Out comes his king pawn. Meet him nose to nose: yours goes two squares too. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Nose to nose. Those two pawns are stuck staring at each other. } (1... c5 { The Sicilian. A great move, and a jungle for another day. } 2. Nf3 { Sharp, famous, and not today. }) (1... e6 { The French. Solid, but your light bishop is locked in for a while. } 2. d4 d5 { A fine defence, and a chapter of its own. }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann. Also good, and also another story. } 2. d4 d5 { Solid chess. Today we answer him right in the middle. }) (1... d5 { Bold. But he simply takes, and your queen must come out early to fetch it. } 2. exd5 { A pawn down, and the queen is running errands on move two. }) 2. Nf3 { His knight leans on your pawn. No panic: send a guard. [%cal Yb8c6] } 2... Nc6 { Guarded, and a piece out. That knight is doing two jobs at once. } (2... Nf6 { Cheeky, and playable. But the pawn on e5 is left to look after itself. } 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 { You do win it back, and that is a whole other lesson. }) (2... d6 { Solid, and it shuts your dark bishop in its own front room. } 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 { He owns the middle and you are living in three rows. }) (2... f6 { Never guard with this one. It opens a road to your own king. } 3. Nxe5 { Take back, and a queen check on h5 collects your rook. }) (2... d5 { The elephant gambit. A pawn given away, and not much coming back. } 3. exd5 e4 4. Qe2 { A pawn up, and the one on e4 is next on his list. }) 3. Bc4 { The Italian. That bishop is staring at f7. Copy him and stare straight back. [%cal Yf8c5] } 3... Bc5 { Now yours glares at f2. Two bishops, two sore spots, and nobody is better. [%cal Gc5f2] } (3... Nf6 { Just as good on the scoreboard. But it invites a very rude knight jump. } 4. Ng5 { Straight at f7. That is the fried liver, and it is no fun from this side. }) (3... d6 { Safe, and rather sleepy. Your best bishop stays behind the door. } 4. O-O Be7 { Playable, but the good diagonal is his now. }) (3... Be7 { The bishop hides, and the middle is his for free. } 4. d4 exd4 { He is better and you have nothing to bite on. }) (3... Na5 { Chasing the bishop with the wrong piece, and e5 is left alone. } 4. Nxe5 { He snaps the pawn up at once. You win the bishop, and he is happier. }) 4. c3 { He is building a big centre. Bring the last knight out and poke at e4. [%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { Out it comes, and his e pawn is suddenly being watched. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... Bb6 { A careful step back before anybody asked for one. } 5. d4 d6 { He builds the big centre for free while you shuffle. }) (4... d6 { Playable, and slow. That knight was itching to come out. } 5. d4 Bb6 { His pawns roll forward and your bishop backs away. }) (4... Qe7 { Your queen parks in her own knight's doorway. } 5. d4 Bb6 { Your pieces are queueing up behind each other. }) (4... Bxf2+ { One check is never worth a bishop. } 5. Kxf2 { His king simply takes it, and you are a whole bishop poorer. }) 5. d3 { He props up his e pawn. Do the same, and let your light bishop out. [%cal Yd7d6] } 5... d6 { The pawn guards e5 and opens the road for your light bishop. [%cal Gc8h3] } (5... O-O { Castling is good too, and we are doing it next move anyway. } 6. O-O d6 { Same position, different move order — nothing lost. }) (5... a6 { A handy little move, and just as good as ours. } 6. O-O Ba7 { Fine chess. We let the bishop out first. }) (5... Bb6 { The bishop retreats before anybody asked it to. } 6. O-O d6 { No harm done, but that was a move spent on nothing. }) (5... Ng4 { The knight leaps at f2, and there is nothing there to hit. } 6. d4 { He kicks your bishop and your knight is out on a limb. }) 6. O-O { His king is tucked in. Time to tuck yours in too. [%cal Ye8g8] } 6... O-O { Both kings safe, every piece out, nothing hanging. A perfect opening. [%csl Gg8] } (6... a6 { A good, useful move. But the king comes first. } 7. a4 O-O { You still end up castled, just one move slower. }) (6... h6 { A useful little pawn move, with your king still in the middle. } 7. b4 Bb6 { He grabs space while your king waits about. }) (6... Bg4 { A real pin, and he simply shrugs it off. } 7. Nbd2 { A pawn to h3 comes next, and your king is still homeless. }) (6... Bb6 { The bishop backs away with nobody chasing it. } 7. Nbd2 h6 { Playable, and your king is still out in the open. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the Spanish, he takes the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/FmyET55b"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The same start. This time, keep an eye on his bishop. }
3. Bb5 { This time his bishop goes hunting on the other wing: the Spanish. It pokes the knight that guards e5. Ask it a question. [%csl Rc6] } 3... a6 { The little pawn asks the big question: take my knight, or step back? [%cal Ga6b5] } (3... Nf6 { This is every bit as good: a normal developing move. } 4. O-O Nxe4 { Playable and fine. But we like asking the bishop the question. }) (3... Bc5 { A natural developing move, and quite playable. } 4. c3 Nf6 { Fine. In our line the bishop gets asked first. }) (3... Be7 { Solid and sleepy. That bishop had a much better square. } 4. c3 Nf6 { You are fine, and he has an easy afternoon. }) (3... Nge7 { This knight parks right in front of your own bishop. } 4. O-O Ng6 { Squashed, and he is comfortably better. }) (3... d6 { Solid, and a bit passive: your dark bishop is locked in again. } 4. Nc3 Nf6 { Playable, and he has the easier game. }) 4. Bxc6 { He takes the guard, so your pawn on e5 is loose. Which pawn takes back? [%csl Re5] } 4... dxc6 { The d pawn, and it opens the queen's road to d4. Let him take on e5 if he dares. [%cal Gd8d4] } (4... bxc6 { The b pawn, and your own d pawn blocks the queen's road. No fork on d4 now. [%csl Rd7] } 5. Nc3 Qf6 6. d4 { With no fork to fear, he leaves e5 alone and builds the middle. }) (4... Nf6 { You forgot the bishop on c6. It is standing there with your knight. } 5. Bd5 c6 6. Bb3 { It simply strolls away, a whole knight up. }) (4... Bc5 { A good square, and you still have not taken that bishop back. } 5. Ba4 Nf6 6. Nxe5 { A knight and a pawn down. Take first, develop after. }) 5. Nxe5 { He grabs the pawn, and that is his mistake — he should have castled instead. Now win it straight back. [%csl Re5,Re4] } 5... Qd4 { There she is. From d4 she pokes the knight and the e pawn at once. [%cal Gd4e5,Gd4e4] } (5... Qe7 { Just as good. From e7 she lines up on the knight and the pawn too. } 6. Nf3 Qxe4+ { The same pawn back, with the same cheeky check. }) (5... Qg5 { Greedy in another direction, and it works too. } 6. Nf3 Qxg2 7. Rg1 { A pawn won, and now your queen is being chased round the garden. }) (5... Nf6 { A fine square for the knight, and your pawn is still missing. } 6. d3 Bc5 7. Nf3 { He keeps it, and you have nothing at all to show. }) (5... Bd6 { You develop politely and let him keep his snack. } 6. Nc4 Bc5 7. d3 { A pawn down for nothing at all. }) (5... Be6 { The bishop comes out, and the pawn is gone for good. } 6. d4 Ne7 7. Be3 { He is a clean pawn up. }) 6. Nf3 { The knight runs for it. Now go and collect. } 6... Qxe4+ { The pawn is back, with check, and you still own both bishops. [%csl Gc8,Gf8] } (6... Qf6 { She steps aside, and the free pawn stays his. } 7. d4 Qg6 8. Nc3 { A pawn was sitting there for the taking and you waved it past. }) (6... Qd6 { Safe, and the whole point was that pawn on e4. } 7. d3 Ne7 8. Nbd2 { He is a pawn up and very comfortable. }) (6... Qd8 { All the way home again, and the whole trip was for nothing. } 7. d4 Ne7 8. Qd3 { A pawn down, with nothing to show. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the Spanish main line"]
[Result "0-1"]
