[Role "exercise"]

{ The same start again. This time, watch his queen pawn. }
3. d4 { His queen pawn charges into the middle. He crashes in and offers a trade. So what do you do about it? } 3... exd4 { Take it. A pawn in your pocket, and he must spend a move fetching it. } (3... Nxd4 { The wrong piece takes, and his queen ends up in the middle for free. } 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. Qxd4 { She sits there on her throne while you are still at home. }) (3... d6 { Too timid. He pushes past and squashes you flat. } 4. d5 Nb8 { Your poor knight trudges all the way home again. }) (3... d5 { Both centres crash, but his knight reaches e5 first. } 4. Nxe5 dxe4 5. Bb5 { He wins the pawn back and there is a check coming. }) (3... Bd6 { The bishop parks in front of your own queen pawn. } 4. c3 exd4 5. cxd4 { He gets the perfect centre and you took the long way round. }) 4. Nxd4 { He fetches it back with the knight. Develop now, and hit something on the way. } 4... Nf6 { Out and poking. That knight is asking his e pawn some hard questions. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... Bc5 { A natural square, and quite playable too. } 5. Nb3 Bb6 { Fine, but he gains time chasing your bishop about. }) (4... Bb4+ { A check that helps him: he blocks it and develops for free. } 5. c3 Bc5 { Your bishop moves twice and he is a move ahead. }) (4... Qf6 { The queen pops out early and he simply chases her. } 5. Nb3 Qg6 { She is safe, and you have lost time. }) (4... d5 { The centre break, one move too early. } 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. Bb5 { His pieces come out with threats attached. }) 5. Nc3 { That knight is the only guard of his e pawn. Can you tie it up? [%csl Re4] } 5... Bb4 { The pin. The knight is stuck, and his e pawn suddenly looks lonely. [%cal Gb4c3,Gf6e4] } (5... Nxe4 { You do win it, for about one second. } 6. Nxe4 Qe7 7. f3 { Nothing gained, and his pieces are happier than yours. }) (5... Bc5 { A natural square. But his knight on d4 is well looked after. } 6. Be3 Bb6 { He is comfortable and you are a shade worse. }) (5... Be7 { Too quiet. Now he trades and drags your king out for a walk. } 6. Nxc6 dxc6 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { No castling ever again. }) (5... a6 { A slow little move in a sharp position. } 6. Bc4 Bb4 { You play the bishop out anyway, a move behind. }) 6. Nxc6 { He trades on c6. Careful: which pawn takes back? Your bishop is watching. [%csl Rb4] } 6... bxc6 { The b pawn this time, and your d pawn stays put on d7. His queen has no road to d8 now, so no forced trade. [%cal Gb7c6] } (6... dxc6 { Natural, and it opens the d file for a trade you do not want. } 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Queens off, no castling, and a long day for your king. }) (6... O-O { You left the knight on c6, and your bishop hanging on b4. } 7. Nxb4 { He takes the bishop and is a whole piece up. }) (6... Nxe4 { One free pawn, one lost bishop. A bad swap. } 7. Nxb4 { The bishop drops and he is a piece ahead. }) (6... Bxc3+ { Playable, and you tidy his pawns up for him. } 7. bxc3 bxc6 { The bishop pair, given away for nothing. }) 0-1


