[Role "exercise"]

{ You met him in the middle again. This time, watch what he tries. }
2. Qh5 { She swings out and glares at your e pawn. Guard it, and develop while you do. } 2... Nc6 { Guard and develop, in one move. That is how you answer a bully. [%csl Ge5] } (2... g6 { Never shoo her with that pawn: it stops guarding e5. } 3. Qxe5+ { Check, and a fork. Your king and the rook in the corner. [%cal Re5h8] }) (2... Nf6 { It develops, and the pawn simply falls with check. } 3. Qxe5+ Be7 { A whole pawn gone, and she is still out there. }) (2... d6 { This guards it too, and it is perfectly good. } 3. Bc4 g6 { A fine game. Our knight guards and develops at once. }) (2... Qe7 { Solid, and it does guard e5. But your queen blocks your bishop. } 3. Bc4 Nf6 { Playable, and a little awkward. }) (2... Bd6 { It guards the pawn, and it stands on your own d pawn's toes. } 3. Bc4 Qe7 { Cramped, and he is slightly better. }) 3. Bc4 { Bishop and queen both stare at f7. That is the four move mate. Stop it. [%csl Rf7] } 3... g6 { The little pawn shoos her away and slams the door on f7. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it is mate. } 4. Qxf7# { The queen lands on f7 with the bishop behind her. Scholar's mate. }) (3... d5 { It hits the bishop, and hands over a pawn and then a knight. } 4. Bxd5 Qe7 5. Bxc6+ { He eats the pawn, then the knight, and says thank you. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending. Your queen covers f7 and you are fine. } 4. Qd1 Nf6 { Solid. Shooing her with the g pawn is more fun. }) (3... Nh6 { It covers f7, and the knight is miserable on the edge. } 4. d3 g6 { You play the pawn move anyway, with a sadder knight. }) 4. Qf3 { She slides over and stares at f7 all over again. Block the road. [%csl Rf7] } 4... Nf6 { The knight steps into the doorway and develops. Her big idea is dead. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, and it is mate on f7. } 5. Qxf7# { The road was still wide open. The knight had to block it. }) (4... Qe7 { It covers f7 and is perfectly good. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Solid. The knight blocks and develops in one go. }) (4... Qf6 { Offering a trade. Fine, but your queen moves twice. } 5. Qd1 Qd8 { Both queens go home and nothing has happened. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, and the knight is sad on the rim. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Playable. The other knight was the good one. }) (4... f5 { It stops the mate, and tears your own front door off. } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Still fine, but that pawn will hurt you one day. }) 5. Ne2 { At last, a piece. The whole queen raid has achieved precisely nothing. } 5... Bg7 { Your bishop takes the road the g pawn opened, all the way to a1. [%cal Gg7a1] } (5... d6 { Just as good, maybe even a shade more precise. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { The same bishop move, one beat later. }) (5... h6 { A quiet little move while your bishop is still waiting to come out. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { You play it anyway, a move behind. }) (5... Na5 { Going after his bishop. Playable, but slow. } 6. d3 Bg7 { The bishop was the piece that mattered. }) (5... a5 { A pawn move with no plan behind it. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { Nothing lost, and nothing gained either. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: he plays the queen pawn"]
