{ Now for the other big opening move. Watch what he opens with this time. }
1. d4 { He plays the queen pawn. Same plan, other side of the board: your queen pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yd7d5] } 1... d5 { Nose to nose again. Nobody is giving away the middle today. } (1... Nf6 { Excellent too, a flexible favourite. But it opens a very long book. } 2. c4 g6 { That is the Indian world. Today we keep it simple. }) (1... e6 { Playable, and it can even transpose. But it shuts your bishop in first. } 2. e4 d5 { Now you are in the French, through the back door. }) (1... c6 { A waiting move. Fine, but that pawn belongs in the middle. } 2. e3 d5 { You play d5 anyway, one move later. }) (1... d6 { Too small a step. He builds a big centre for free. } 2. e4 Nf6 { He owns the middle and you must fight your way in. }) 2. c4 { The queens gambit. A pawn offered, to lure your centre pawn away. Prop it up. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { Propped up. Your pawn on d5 is going nowhere, and your bishop wakes next. } (2... dxc4 { You may take it. You will not be allowed to keep it. } 3. e4 { He builds a huge centre and collects the pawn later. }) (2... c6 { The Slav. A very good move, and a whole other chapter. } 3. Nf3 Nf6 { Solid stuff. Ours is the one with e6. }) (2... Nc6 { This knight stands on your own c pawn's square. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. e3 { His centre is bigger and your queen came out early. }) (2... c5 { Too sharp too soon. He takes, and your centre falls apart. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 { Your queen gets chased and his pieces fly out. }) 3. Nc3 { He develops and leans on d5. Send out the knight that helps guard it. [%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { Out it comes, and d5 has a friend. } (3... c6 { The Semi-Slav. Just as good, and a whole chapter of its own. } 4. e3 Nf6 { You bring the knight out anyway, one move later. }) (3... Bb4 { This pin is just as good. But it is a sharper road. } 4. e3 Nf6 { The same knight move by a rougher path. }) (3... c5 { The pawn break, and far too early. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { His pieces come out faster and your d pawn needs babysitting. }) (3... a6 { A slow little move while the centre is burning. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { He is comfortable and you gained nothing. }) (3... Be7 { Fine, just one move early. The knight comes first. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 { Playable, and he takes the initiative. }) 4. Bg5 { That bishop pins your knight to your queen. Put your own bishop in the way. [%cal Yf8e7] } 4... Be7 { The bishop steps into the gap, and your knight can breathe again. [%cal Ge7g5] } (4... dxc4 { Grabbing the pawn now is also playable. } 5. e3 c5 { A real line, and a great deal more to remember. }) (4... Bb4 { A pin against a pin. Sharp, and just as good. } 5. e3 c5 { Playable, and ours is the calm one. }) (4... c6 { Solid, and a touch passive. } 5. e3 Nbd7 { Fine, but the bishop wanted to come out first. }) (4... Nbd7 { Also solid. The new knight guards the pinned one, which is the real point. } 5. cxd5 exd5 { Playable. We answer the pin with the bishop. }) 5. e3 { A small, solid wall. Your king has waited long enough: send him home. [%cal Ye8g8] } 5... O-O { King safe, pieces out, pawns tidy. That is a fine opening. [%csl Gg8] } (5... h6 { Asking the bishop first is also very good. } 6. Bh4 O-O { Either way, you end up just as safely castled. }) (5... Nbd7 { A good square, and just as good. But the king comes first. } 6. Nf3 O-O { You reach the same safe position by a different road. }) (5... b6 { Fine, and a little slow with your king still in the middle. } 6. cxd5 Nxd5 { Playable, and he is a shade better. }) (5... dxc4 { Taking now hands him the centre and a free move. } 6. Bxc4 a6 { He is delighted: his bishop came out with a tempo. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the London bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/w8eQu5SA"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq d6 0 2"]
