[Role "exercise"]

{ You have met him in the middle. Now watch where his dark bishop goes. }
2. Bf4 { The London. That bishop is his pride and joy. Get a piece out and stay calm. } 2... Nf6 { The knight comes out and takes the squares his bishop is dreaming of. } (2... c5 { A fine punch at his centre. } 3. e3 Nf6 { Also good. We develop first and keep it simple. }) (2... e6 { Playable, and it shuts your light bishop in a little early. } 3. e3 c5 { Fine. The knight first keeps more doors open. }) (2... Bf5 { Also fine: this bishop gets out before your own pawns lock it in. } 3. c4 e6 { A real line, and he pokes at your centre at once. }) (2... Nc6 { Natural, and it stands on your own c pawn's square. } 3. e3 Nf6 { He is comfortable, and your c pawn has nowhere to go. }) 3. e3 { Up goes his little pyramid. Now open a door for your own dark bishop. } 3... e6 { The door is open. Your dark bishop can come out and meet his. } (3... c5 { A good hit at d4. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 { Also fine. Ours goes after his bishop instead. }) (3... g6 { A different plan, and quite playable. } 4. c3 c5 { Fine chess, but our bishop wants d6, not g7. }) (3... Bf5 { Out before the pawn, and he trades it off. } 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. cxd3 { His pawns get better and yours do not. }) (3... Bg4 { There is no knight there to pin. } 4. Qd3 { His queen comes out looking at b5 and b7. }) 4. Nf3 { Nearly everything is out, and that bishop on f4 is the best piece he owns. } 4... Bd6 { Offer the swap: his favourite bishop for your sleepy one. What a deal. [%cal Gd6f4] } (4... c5 { A fine move, hitting the centre at once. } 5. c3 Nc6 { Also good. Trading his best bishop is our idea. }) (4... Be7 { Safe, and it lets his bishop live a long and happy life. } 5. c4 O-O { Perfectly playable, and he keeps the better bishop. }) (4... a6 { A little waiting move. Nothing wrong, nothing gained. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { He develops in peace. }) (4... Bd7 { The bishop has nothing to do on that square. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { Playable, but the other bishop was the important one. }) 5. Bxd6 { He takes. Now take back, and think hard about which one does it. } 5... cxd6 { The c pawn, opening a road for your rook and pointing at the middle. [%csl Gc8] } (5... Qxd6 { Also completely fine, and worth exactly the same. } 6. c4 dxc4 { A good game. The pawn recapture leaves your queen free. }) (5... Ne4 { You left the bishop standing there. } 6. Ba3 { It strolls away to a3, and you are a whole piece down. }) (5... Nc6 { Developing is lovely, and that bishop is still standing there. } 6. Ba3 { A whole bishop, gone for nothing. }) 0-1
